From 88f6b92a428411d00808bfcbc1e12f097bbb9463 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Nelson <will.nelson@discordapp.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Apr 2026 22:59:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wire: add fuel accounting to ParseResult; plugin-runtime:
 meter query work with fuel
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

QueryCursor is O(n^2) on patterns rooted at left-recursive AST nodes
(kotlin/swift navigation_expression, javascript member_expression). At
~16 KB of attacker-shaped input ('a' + '.b()'.repeat(4000)) a single
parse_raw call takes ~3 s, freezing the host renderer.

Bound that with *fuel* — a model of the query work done — rather than
elapsed wall-clock time:

1. arborium-wire: add 'fuel_used: u32' and 'out_of_fuel: bool' to
   Utf8ParseResult and Utf16ParseResult. Required (not
   #[serde(default)]) because the downstream consumer always ships in
   lockstep with the producer.

2. arborium-plugin-runtime: install a fuel meter via tree-sitter's
   ts_query_cursor_exec_with_options progress callback. The callback
   fires inside the cursor's hot loop, interrupting the actual O(n^2)
   work — unlike the existing 'cancelled' AtomicBool flag, which is
   checked between matches and can't fire while one match.next() call
   is grinding through quadratic state-pool transitions.

   tree-sitter consults the callback once per 100 operations of its
   advance loop (OP_COUNT_PER_QUERY_CALLBACK_CHECK in query.c), so each
   tick bills for the 100 operations that just ran (OPS_PER_TICK),
   each priced by a cost model of the advance loop: FUEL_PER_OPERATION
   for stepping the cursor plus FUEL_PER_LIVE_STATE per in-progress
   match it had to touch, read from the state count the previous patch
   publishes. The closure captures a Cell<u32> by reference and Breaks
   once the allotment is spent; after the matches loop, parse_raw reads
   the cell and bubbles the cost up
   through parse() / parse_utf16() into ParseResult.fuel_used /
   .out_of_fuel. When the cursor is cut off it returns whatever spans it
   produced so far — partial highlights instead of a multi-second
   freeze.

   The 150:1 ratio between those two terms was fitted by measurement:
   milliseconds-per-fuel then holds within ~2x across everything from
   small source files to the chain bombs, where charging per tick alone
   is off by 70x (the attack's operations are few but each is enormous)
   and charging per state-visit alone is off by 130x the other way.

   Fuel rather than time buys three things. It's deterministic: a given
   (query, tree) pair always costs the same, so the cutoff can't move
   under a loaded CPU, a throttled background tab, or a debugger pause,
   and tests can assert on it exactly. It's divisible: parse_with_fuel /
   parse_utf16_with_fuel take the allotment as an argument, so a caller
   highlighting one document across several grammars (primary parse plus
   injections) can thread a single pool through every query and bound
   the document as a whole — a per-call time budget instead lets N
   injected blocks cost N x the budget. And it drops the std::time
   dependency, so the wasm host no longer needs clock_gettime.

   The trade is that fuel bounds work, not seconds: a slower machine
   takes longer to burn the same allotment rather than truncating
   sooner. Sizing the allotment for the slowest host that matters is the
   caller's job — see arborium_rt::highlight::HIGHLIGHT_FUEL.

Grammar-agnostic: any future O(n^2) query, in any language, is
bounded by the same primitive.
---
diff --git a/crates/arborium-plugin-runtime/src/lib.rs b/crates/arborium-plugin-runtime/src/lib.rs
index abf0eee..0d1f176 100644
--- a/crates/arborium-plugin-runtime/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/arborium-plugin-runtime/src/lib.rs
@@ -48,10 +48,13 @@ use arborium_sysroot as _;
 use alloc::collections::BTreeMap;
 use alloc::string::String;
 use alloc::vec::Vec;
+use core::cell::Cell;
+use core::ops::ControlFlow;
 use core::sync::atomic::{AtomicBool, AtomicU32, Ordering};
 
 use arborium_tree_sitter::{
-    InputEdit, Language, Parser, Point, Query, QueryCursor, QueryError, StreamingIterator, Tree,
+    InputEdit, Language, Parser, Point, Query, QueryCursor, QueryCursorOptions, QueryCursorState,
+    QueryError, StreamingIterator, Tree,
 };
 use arborium_wire::{
     Edit, ParseError, Utf8Injection, Utf8ParseResult, Utf8Span, Utf16Injection, Utf16ParseResult,
@@ -235,6 +238,63 @@ pub struct PluginRuntime {
     next_session_id: AtomicU32,
 }
 
+/// Query-cursor operations that have run between two ticks of the progress
+/// callback.
+///
+/// tree-sitter consults the callback once every
+/// `OP_COUNT_PER_QUERY_CALLBACK_CHECK` (= 100) operations of its advance
+/// loop (`query.c`), so that is the granularity at which fuel can be
+/// metered: each tick bills for the 100 operations just executed.
+pub const OPS_PER_TICK: u32 = 100;
+
+/// Fuel charged for one cursor operation, independent of how many matches
+/// are in flight — the fixed cost of stepping the tree cursor and testing
+/// the current node.
+///
+/// Together with [`FUEL_PER_LIVE_STATE`] this is a cost model for
+/// `ts_query_cursor__advance`, whose real cost per operation is
+/// `base + per_state × live states`: the loop touches every in-progress
+/// match on every step. The ratio 150:1 was fitted by timing both extremes
+/// in the browser runtime — ordinary source files, which keep only a
+/// handful of states alive (so almost all of their cost is the base term),
+/// against the quadratic chain-bomb shapes this budget exists to stop,
+/// which keep thousands alive (so almost all of theirs is the per-state
+/// term). Under the combined model, milliseconds-per-fuel varies about 2×
+/// across that whole range; charging by operations alone or by state-visits
+/// alone is off by 70× and 130× respectively, which would make any single
+/// cutoff either useless against the attack or ruinous for large ordinary
+/// files.
+pub const FUEL_PER_OPERATION: u32 = 150;
+
+/// Fuel charged per in-progress query state a cursor operation touches.
+/// The unit of fuel, in other words, is one state-visit. See
+/// [`FUEL_PER_OPERATION`].
+pub const FUEL_PER_LIVE_STATE: u32 = 1;
+
+/// Fuel allotted to a bare [`PluginRuntime::parse`] /
+/// [`PluginRuntime::parse_utf16`] call, i.e. one query execution with no
+/// caller-supplied budget.
+///
+/// Sized so that legitimate source files finish comfortably while the
+/// quadratic-query blowup below is cut off — see
+/// `arborium_rt::highlight::HIGHLIGHT_FUEL`, which uses the same figure for
+/// a whole document. Callers running a multi-grammar pipeline (a primary
+/// parse plus injections) should allot one shared pool across the document
+/// via [`PluginRuntime::parse_with_fuel`] rather than taking this default
+/// per call, so total cost is bounded no matter how many injected blocks
+/// the document contains.
+pub const DEFAULT_QUERY_FUEL: u32 = 80_000_000;
+
+/// What one query execution cost, and whether it finished.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct FuelSpent {
+    /// Fuel consumed. May overshoot the allotment by up to one tick's
+    /// worth, since the cursor is only interruptible at tick boundaries.
+    pub used: u32,
+    /// True if the allotment ran out and the cursor was cut off mid-query.
+    pub out_of_fuel: bool,
+}
+
 impl PluginRuntime {
     /// Create a new plugin runtime with the given highlight configuration.
     pub fn new(config: HighlightConfig) -> Self {
@@ -312,10 +372,21 @@ impl PluginRuntime {
     }
 
     /// Internal: execute query and collect raw spans/injections with byte offsets.
+    ///
+    /// `fuel` bounds how much query-cursor work this call may do; the
+    /// returned [`FuelSpent`] reports what it actually cost and whether the
+    /// cursor was cut off. A cut-off cursor yields partial output — whatever
+    /// spans and injections it had produced when fuel ran dry.
     fn parse_raw(
         &mut self,
         session_id: u32,
-    ) -> Result<(String, Vec<RawSpan>, Vec<RawInjection>), ParseError> {
+        fuel: u32,
+    ) -> Result<(String, Vec<RawSpan>, Vec<RawInjection>, FuelSpent), ParseError> {
+        const NO_FUEL_SPENT: FuelSpent = FuelSpent {
+            used: 0,
+            out_of_fuel: false,
+        };
+
         let session = self
             .sessions
             .get_mut(&session_id)
@@ -323,7 +394,7 @@ impl PluginRuntime {
 
         // Check for cancellation
         if session.cancelled.load(Ordering::Relaxed) {
-            return Ok((String::new(), Vec::new(), Vec::new()));
+            return Ok((String::new(), Vec::new(), Vec::new(), NO_FUEL_SPENT));
         }
 
         let tree = session
@@ -338,8 +409,59 @@ impl PluginRuntime {
         let source = text.as_bytes();
         let root = tree.root_node();
 
+        // Fuel budget for query execution.
+        //
+        // tree-sitter's QueryCursor is O(n^2) on patterns rooted at left-
+        // recursive AST nodes (kotlin/swift navigation_expression,
+        // javascript member_expression, etc.). At 16 KB of attacker-shaped
+        // input — `a.b().b().b()…` — a single query takes ~3 s, freezing
+        // the host renderer. The progress callback below fires inside
+        // ts_query_cursor_exec's hot loop, so it can interrupt the actual
+        // work — unlike the cooperative `cancelled` flag below, which is
+        // checked between matches and never gets a chance to fire while
+        // one match.next() call is grinding through quadratic state-pool
+        // transitions.
+        //
+        // The meter is modelled query work, not elapsed time. That buys
+        // determinism — a given (query, tree) pair always costs the same
+        // fuel, so the cutoff is reproducible across machines and immune to
+        // a loaded CPU or a debugger pause — and divisibility: because the
+        // caller passes the allotment in, one pool can cover the many query
+        // executions that make up a single document (primary parse plus
+        // injections). The trade is that a slower machine takes longer to
+        // burn the same fuel rather than truncating sooner; the budget bounds
+        // work, and it is up to the caller's allotment to keep that work
+        // small enough to be a bounded amount of time on the slowest host it
+        // cares about.
+        let spent = Cell::new(0u32);
+        let spent_ref = &spent;
+        let mut burn_fuel = move |state: &QueryCursorState| -> ControlFlow<()> {
+            // Bill the 100 operations that ran since the last tick, each at
+            // the cost model's rate for however many matches were in flight.
+            // Saturating throughout: a deep enough chain bomb overflows u32
+            // fuel, and pinning at the ceiling is exactly the right answer
+            // there — it is well past any allotment a caller would grant.
+            let live_states = state.in_progress_state_count() as u32;
+            let per_op =
+                FUEL_PER_OPERATION.saturating_add(FUEL_PER_LIVE_STATE.saturating_mul(live_states));
+            let used = spent_ref
+                .get()
+                .saturating_add(OPS_PER_TICK.saturating_mul(per_op));
+            spent_ref.set(used);
+            if used >= fuel {
+                ControlFlow::Break(())
+            } else {
+                ControlFlow::Continue(())
+            }
+        };
+
         // Execute the query using streaming iterator
-        let mut matches = session.cursor.matches(&self.config.query, root, source);
+        let mut matches = session.cursor.matches_with_options(
+            &self.config.query,
+            root,
+            source,
+            QueryCursorOptions::new().progress_callback(&mut burn_fuel),
+        );
 
         let mut check_count = 0;
         const CANCELLATION_CHECK_INTERVAL: usize = 100;
@@ -350,7 +472,7 @@ impl PluginRuntime {
             if check_count >= CANCELLATION_CHECK_INTERVAL {
                 check_count = 0;
                 if session.cancelled.load(Ordering::Relaxed) {
-                    return Ok((String::new(), Vec::new(), Vec::new()));
+                    return Ok((String::new(), Vec::new(), Vec::new(), NO_FUEL_SPENT));
                 }
             }
 
@@ -431,7 +553,16 @@ impl PluginRuntime {
             }
         }
 
-        Ok((text, raw_spans, raw_injections))
+        let used = spent.get();
+        Ok((
+            text,
+            raw_spans,
+            raw_injections,
+            FuelSpent {
+                used,
+                out_of_fuel: used >= fuel,
+            },
+        ))
     }
 
     /// Parse the current text and return spans and injections with UTF-8 byte offsets.
@@ -440,8 +571,25 @@ impl PluginRuntime {
     /// UTF-8 byte boundaries.
     ///
     /// If cancelled, returns an empty result.
+    ///
+    /// Runs on [`DEFAULT_QUERY_FUEL`]; use [`Self::parse_with_fuel`] to
+    /// share one budget across several parses.
     pub fn parse(&mut self, session_id: u32) -> Result<Utf8ParseResult, ParseError> {
-        let (_text, raw_spans, raw_injections) = self.parse_raw(session_id)?;
+        self.parse_with_fuel(session_id, DEFAULT_QUERY_FUEL)
+    }
+
+    /// [`Self::parse`], with an explicit fuel allotment.
+    ///
+    /// The result's `fuel_used` / `out_of_fuel` report what the query cost
+    /// and whether it was cut off. Callers highlighting a document made of
+    /// several languages should thread one pool through every call
+    /// (`pool -= result.fuel_used`) so the document as a whole is bounded.
+    pub fn parse_with_fuel(
+        &mut self,
+        session_id: u32,
+        fuel: u32,
+    ) -> Result<Utf8ParseResult, ParseError> {
+        let (_text, raw_spans, raw_injections, spent) = self.parse_raw(session_id, fuel)?;
 
         // Convert to UTF-8 spans (just cast the byte offsets)
         let mut spans: Vec<Utf8Span> = raw_spans
@@ -468,7 +616,12 @@ impl PluginRuntime {
             })
             .collect();
 
-        Ok(Utf8ParseResult { spans, injections })
+        Ok(Utf8ParseResult {
+            spans,
+            injections,
+            fuel_used: spent.used,
+            out_of_fuel: spent.out_of_fuel,
+        })
     }
 
     /// Parse the current text and return spans and injections with UTF-16 code unit indices.
@@ -477,11 +630,28 @@ impl PluginRuntime {
     /// DOM APIs use UTF-16 code unit indices.
     ///
     /// If cancelled, returns an empty result.
+    ///
+    /// Runs on [`DEFAULT_QUERY_FUEL`]; use [`Self::parse_utf16_with_fuel`]
+    /// to share one budget across several parses.
     pub fn parse_utf16(&mut self, session_id: u32) -> Result<Utf16ParseResult, ParseError> {
-        let (text, raw_spans, raw_injections) = self.parse_raw(session_id)?;
+        self.parse_utf16_with_fuel(session_id, DEFAULT_QUERY_FUEL)
+    }
+
+    /// [`Self::parse_utf16`], with an explicit fuel allotment. See
+    /// [`Self::parse_with_fuel`].
+    pub fn parse_utf16_with_fuel(
+        &mut self,
+        session_id: u32,
+        fuel: u32,
+    ) -> Result<Utf16ParseResult, ParseError> {
+        let (text, raw_spans, raw_injections, spent) = self.parse_raw(session_id, fuel)?;
 
         if raw_spans.is_empty() && raw_injections.is_empty() {
-            return Ok(Utf16ParseResult::empty());
+            return Ok(Utf16ParseResult {
+                fuel_used: spent.used,
+                out_of_fuel: spent.out_of_fuel,
+                ..Utf16ParseResult::empty()
+            });
         }
 
         // Collect all byte offsets and batch convert to UTF-16
@@ -533,7 +703,12 @@ impl PluginRuntime {
             })
             .collect();
 
-        Ok(Utf16ParseResult { spans, injections })
+        Ok(Utf16ParseResult {
+            spans,
+            injections,
+            fuel_used: spent.used,
+            out_of_fuel: spent.out_of_fuel,
+        })
     }
 
     /// Get the language provided by this plugin.
diff --git a/crates/arborium-wire/src/lib.rs b/crates/arborium-wire/src/lib.rs
index 8124483..ef0fee9 100644
--- a/crates/arborium-wire/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/arborium-wire/src/lib.rs
@@ -79,6 +79,21 @@ pub struct Utf8ParseResult {
     pub spans: Vec<Utf8Span>,
     /// Injection points for other languages.
     pub injections: Vec<Utf8Injection>,
+    /// Query fuel consumed by this parse.
+    ///
+    /// Fuel models the work tree-sitter's query cursor did — operations
+    /// weighted by how many candidate matches each one had to touch —
+    /// rather than how long it took, so a given (query, tree) pair always
+    /// costs the same amount, on any machine. Charged in blocks of 100
+    /// operations (the cursor's progress-callback interval), so a document
+    /// too small to reach the first block reports 0.
+    pub fuel_used: u32,
+    /// True if the fuel the caller allotted ran out before the QueryCursor
+    /// finished. `spans` and `injections` then hold whatever was collected
+    /// before fuel ran dry — partial output. The caller can use this to
+    /// surface "highlighting was interrupted" in UI or telemetry, or to
+    /// fall back to a different highlighter.
+    pub out_of_fuel: bool,
 }
 
 impl Utf8ParseResult {
@@ -87,6 +102,8 @@ impl Utf8ParseResult {
         Self {
             spans: Vec::new(),
             injections: Vec::new(),
+            fuel_used: 0,
+            out_of_fuel: false,
         }
     }
 }
@@ -137,6 +154,10 @@ pub struct Utf16ParseResult {
     pub spans: Vec<Utf16Span>,
     /// Injection points for other languages.
     pub injections: Vec<Utf16Injection>,
+    /// See [`Utf8ParseResult::fuel_used`].
+    pub fuel_used: u32,
+    /// See [`Utf8ParseResult::out_of_fuel`].
+    pub out_of_fuel: bool,
 }
 
 impl Utf16ParseResult {
@@ -145,6 +166,8 @@ impl Utf16ParseResult {
         Self {
             spans: Vec::new(),
             injections: Vec::new(),
+            fuel_used: 0,
+            out_of_fuel: false,
         }
     }
 }
-- 
